1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,049
This bag weighs about ninety pounds.

2
00:00:04,099 --> 00:00:08,217
It's rawhide, sewn shut, and the
seams were sealed with grease.

3
00:00:08,267 --> 00:00:09,809
Inside is food.

4
00:00:10,039 --> 00:00:13,831
Packed like this and kept
dry, it could last for years.

5
00:00:15,295 --> 00:00:16,570
Two hundred years ago,

6
00:00:16,610 --> 00:00:20,747
bags like this crossed the northern
half of a continent by canoe.

7
00:00:20,797 --> 00:00:25,321
And on the eighth of January,
1814, the governor of a small,

8
00:00:25,361 --> 00:00:28,212
hungry colony on the Red River made it

9
00:00:28,252 --> 00:00:31,773
illegal to carry them
out of his territory.

10
00:00:33,137 --> 00:00:34,791
Not illegal to eat.

11
00:00:34,841 --> 00:00:36,293
Illegal to move.

12
00:00:38,857 --> 00:00:40,551
Two and a half years later,

13
00:00:40,591 --> 00:00:45,337
more than twenty men lay dead on a
plain just downriver from his fort.

14
00:00:45,387 --> 00:00:50,428
It was the bloodiest evening of what
historians now call the Pemmican War.

15
00:00:51,992 --> 00:00:54,142
The food in the bag is pemmican.

16
00:00:54,192 --> 00:00:56,902
To understand why people
would fight over it,

17
00:00:56,942 --> 00:01:01,713
you have to understand the problem it
solved, and who knew how to make it.

18
00:01:09,177 --> 00:01:11,375
Fresh meat is mostly water.

19
00:01:11,425 --> 00:01:14,679
That's what makes it heavy,
and that's what makes it rot.

20
00:01:14,729 --> 00:01:16,270
On the northern plains,

21
00:01:16,310 --> 00:01:18,745
a good bison hunt could bring in far

22
00:01:18,785 --> 00:01:22,473
more meat than anyone could
eat before it spoiled.

23
00:01:22,523 --> 00:01:24,844
The hard part was never the hunt.

24
00:01:24,894 --> 00:01:27,289
It was keeping what the hunt provided.

25
00:01:28,253 --> 00:01:30,931
The fur traders didn't solve that problem.

26
00:01:30,981 --> 00:01:34,518
They learned the solution from
the people who already had it.

27
00:01:34,568 --> 00:01:38,205
Plains peoples, among them
the Cree and the Assiniboine,

28
00:01:38,245 --> 00:01:43,749
whose lands met the fur trade along these
rivers, made a food that could travel.

29
00:01:43,799 --> 00:01:48,591
The English word comes from Cree:
pimîhkân, built on the word for fat,

30
00:01:48,631 --> 00:01:49,571
or grease.

31
00:01:51,435 --> 00:01:54,860
The lean meat was cut into
thin sheets and dried,

32
00:01:54,900 --> 00:02:00,171
in the sun and wind or over a slow
fire, until it was hard and brittle.

33
00:02:00,221 --> 00:02:03,023
Then it was pounded,
often on a spread hide,

34
00:02:03,063 --> 00:02:06,931
until it broke down into
something close to coarse meal.

35
00:02:06,981 --> 00:02:09,416
Fat from the same animal was rendered,

36
00:02:09,456 --> 00:02:11,695
and the hot fat was poured over the

37
00:02:11,735 --> 00:02:15,046
pounded meat and worked
all the way through it.

38
00:02:15,096 --> 00:02:19,827
Sometimes dried fruit went in,
saskatoon berries or chokecherries.

39
00:02:19,877 --> 00:02:23,920
The mixture was packed into a
rawhide bag while it was still warm,

40
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:25,819
and the bag was sewn shut.

41
00:02:27,483 --> 00:02:29,884
Most of that work was done by women.

42
00:02:29,934 --> 00:02:34,417
Their skill decided whether a
summer's hunt became a year of food,

43
00:02:34,457 --> 00:02:36,489
or a pile of spoiled meat.

44
00:02:38,053 --> 00:02:39,697
Every step had a job.

45
00:02:39,747 --> 00:02:44,105
Drying removed the water that
bacteria and mold need to grow.

46
00:02:44,155 --> 00:02:49,655
It took five or six pounds of fresh, lean
meat to make a single pound of dried,

47
00:02:49,695 --> 00:02:53,382
pounded meat, so a heavy
load became a light one.

48
00:02:54,346 --> 00:02:56,165
The fat did two things.

49
00:02:56,215 --> 00:03:01,320
It added energy, more than twice as
much per ounce as the meat itself.

50
00:03:01,370 --> 00:03:06,629
And as it cooled, it locked the fibers
in place and kept out air and moisture.

51
00:03:06,679 --> 00:03:10,573
The bag, greased along its
seams, sealed the whole package.

52
00:03:12,037 --> 00:03:14,950
None of this made pemmican indestructible.

53
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,345
Pemmican could go rancid,
and damp could ruin it.

54
00:03:18,395 --> 00:03:22,379
But packed well and kept dry,
a bag could last for years.

55
00:03:22,429 --> 00:03:26,659
What came out was dense, durable
food that could be eaten cold,

56
00:03:26,699 --> 00:03:31,491
straight from the bag, by someone
who had no time to stop and cook.

57
00:03:36,655 --> 00:03:39,094
And at the start of the eighteen-hundreds,

58
00:03:39,134 --> 00:03:43,397
there was an entire industry made
of people who had no time to stop.

59
00:03:44,361 --> 00:03:48,133
The North West Company, run
by merchants in Montreal,

60
00:03:48,173 --> 00:03:51,010
wanted the finest furs on the continent,

61
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:56,623
and some of the best came from the
Athabasca country in the far northwest.

62
00:03:56,673 --> 00:04:00,343
The canoe route there ran
close to three thousand miles.

63
00:04:00,393 --> 00:04:04,255
It was so long that the company
split the journey in two,

64
00:04:04,295 --> 00:04:08,979
with crews from Montreal and crews
from the interior meeting halfway,

65
00:04:09,019 --> 00:04:11,733
at the western end of Lake Superior.

66
00:04:13,097 --> 00:04:15,039
The northern season was short.

67
00:04:15,089 --> 00:04:18,777
Canoes had to get in and get
out before the rivers froze.

68
00:04:18,827 --> 00:04:21,200
Every pound of food on board was a pound

69
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:24,385
of trade goods or furs
that couldn't be carried.

70
00:04:24,435 --> 00:04:28,547
And a crew that stopped to hunt
was a crew that wasn't paddling.

71
00:04:29,511 --> 00:04:31,283
Pemmican changed that math.

72
00:04:31,333 --> 00:04:34,420
Fur-trade accounts reckoned
that around two pounds

73
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:37,024
a day could keep a working man going.

74
00:04:37,074 --> 00:04:39,981
So the company built a
supply chain around it.

75
00:04:40,031 --> 00:04:45,157
Pemmican was made on the plains, bought
from Indigenous and Métis hunters,

76
00:04:45,197 --> 00:04:48,309
and stockpiled at depots along the route.

77
00:04:48,359 --> 00:04:53,695
A northern canoe with a crew of six,
making perhaps thirty-five miles a day,

78
00:04:53,735 --> 00:04:57,946
would take on about four
ninety-pound bags at one depot.

79
00:04:57,996 --> 00:05:02,834
That was enough to carry the crew to
the next depot, hundreds of miles away.

80
00:05:04,598 --> 00:05:08,319
A large share of that
pemmican came from the Métis,

81
00:05:08,359 --> 00:05:11,933
a people whose roots lay
in the fur trade itself,

82
00:05:11,973 --> 00:05:16,353
in families formed by traders
and Cree and Ojibwe women.

83
00:05:16,403 --> 00:05:18,527
By the early eighteen-hundreds,

84
00:05:18,567 --> 00:05:22,296
Métis communities along the
Red and Assiniboine rivers

85
00:05:22,336 --> 00:05:25,657
had their own customs
and their own economy.

86
00:05:25,707 --> 00:05:30,695
Each year, whole families went out
onto the plains together for the hunt,

87
00:05:30,735 --> 00:05:36,220
organized almost like a moving town,
with elected captains and strict rules.

88
00:05:36,270 --> 00:05:40,260
What they brought back, dried and
packed, was sold to the posts.

89
00:05:42,024 --> 00:05:46,066
Historians who study this trade
put it plainly: without cheap,

90
00:05:46,106 --> 00:05:48,305
abundant pemmican from the plains,

91
00:05:48,345 --> 00:05:52,809
the fur trade of the far northwest
would not have been possible.

92
00:05:52,859 --> 00:05:54,838
The furs came from the north.

93
00:05:54,888 --> 00:05:57,209
The fuel came from bison country.

94
00:05:58,773 --> 00:06:02,325
The two great fur companies
weren't equally exposed.

95
00:06:02,375 --> 00:06:05,012
The Hudson's Bay Company
could ship goods by

96
00:06:05,052 --> 00:06:07,751
sea straight into the
heart of the continent,

97
00:06:07,791 --> 00:06:09,197
through Hudson Bay.

98
00:06:09,247 --> 00:06:12,240
The North West Company
had no such shortcut.

99
00:06:12,290 --> 00:06:15,996
Its lifeline was the long
canoe road from Montreal,

100
00:06:16,036 --> 00:06:18,490
and that road ran on pemmican.

101
00:06:18,540 --> 00:06:22,638
Much of that pemmican came down
rivers that met at one place:

102
00:06:22,678 --> 00:06:26,455
the point where the Assiniboine
River joins the Red.

103
00:06:31,019 --> 00:06:36,002
In 1811, the Hudson's Bay Company
granted a Scottish aristocrat,

104
00:06:36,042 --> 00:06:37,572
the Earl of Selkirk,

105
00:06:37,612 --> 00:06:43,278
a tract of land of about a hundred
and sixteen thousand square miles.

106
00:06:43,328 --> 00:06:45,617
That's roughly the size of Italy.

107
00:06:45,667 --> 00:06:49,476
Selkirk and his relatives had
bought heavily into the company,

108
00:06:49,516 --> 00:06:52,208
and he meant to fill
this land with farmers,

109
00:06:52,248 --> 00:06:55,043
many of them from the Scottish Highlands.

110
00:06:55,093 --> 00:06:56,833
He called it Assiniboia.

111
00:06:56,883 --> 00:07:00,798
Right in the middle of it sat
the junction of those two rivers,

112
00:07:00,838 --> 00:07:03,662
and the North West Company's supply line.

113
00:07:04,626 --> 00:07:10,189
The first settlers arrived in 1812,
with a governor named Miles Macdonell.

114
00:07:10,239 --> 00:07:13,235
Farming at Red River
got off to a hard start,

115
00:07:13,275 --> 00:07:17,371
and the colony leaned on bison
meat it could hunt or buy.

116
00:07:17,421 --> 00:07:22,957
By the winter of 1813, food was short,
and more families were on the way.

117
00:07:24,521 --> 00:07:29,082
On January eighth, 1814,
Macdonell issued his order.

118
00:07:29,132 --> 00:07:34,013
For one year, no provisions gathered
in Assiniboia could be taken out of it.

119
00:07:34,063 --> 00:07:37,765
Not meat, not fish, not
grain, not vegetables.

120
00:07:37,815 --> 00:07:41,345
Traders could keep what they
needed to reach their own posts,

121
00:07:41,385 --> 00:07:43,567
with a license from the governor.

122
00:07:43,617 --> 00:07:46,839
Anything else could be
seized, and would be paid for.

123
00:07:48,803 --> 00:07:51,694
On paper, it was about feeding a colony.

124
00:07:51,744 --> 00:07:52,516
In practice,

125
00:07:52,556 --> 00:07:57,458
it landed on the one product the North
West Company could not do without,

126
00:07:57,498 --> 00:08:02,555
and on the Métis families whose living
came from making and selling it.

127
00:08:02,605 --> 00:08:03,063
To them,

128
00:08:03,103 --> 00:08:05,614
it looked less like famine relief than an

129
00:08:05,654 --> 00:08:08,953
attempt to control their
country and their trade.

130
00:08:09,003 --> 00:08:11,558
Both of those things
could be true at once.

131
00:08:13,322 --> 00:08:15,920
That spring, the colony enforced it.

132
00:08:15,970 --> 00:08:18,818
A sheriff named John Spencer was sent to

133
00:08:18,858 --> 00:08:22,429
a North West Company
post on the Souris River.

134
00:08:22,479 --> 00:08:26,843
His party took four hundred and
seventy-nine bags of pemmican,

135
00:08:26,883 --> 00:08:30,613
along with kegs of fat
and bundles of dried meat.

136
00:08:30,663 --> 00:08:33,562
That's more than forty
thousand pounds of food,

137
00:08:33,602 --> 00:08:37,667
taken from a company that needed
it to move its canoes north.

138
00:08:42,731 --> 00:08:44,934
The North West Company answered.

139
00:08:44,984 --> 00:08:49,619
Over the next year, its people
worked to break the colony apart,

140
00:08:49,659 --> 00:08:53,342
and in June of 1815, most
of the settlers left.

141
00:08:53,392 --> 00:08:56,929
Macdonell himself was
taken east under arrest.

142
00:08:56,979 --> 00:08:59,427
For a moment, the colony was gone.

143
00:09:00,391 --> 00:09:01,875
It didn't stay gone.

144
00:09:01,925 --> 00:09:06,358
Settlers returned that same year,
and a new governor, Robert Semple,

145
00:09:06,398 --> 00:09:07,963
arrived in the fall.

146
00:09:08,013 --> 00:09:09,309
In March of 1816,

147
00:09:09,349 --> 00:09:14,575
the colony's men seized the North
West Company's post at the Forks,

148
00:09:14,615 --> 00:09:18,088
Fort Gibraltar, and later pulled it down.

149
00:09:19,652 --> 00:09:23,897
By then, the Métis had a leader
of their own: Cuthbert Grant,

150
00:09:23,937 --> 00:09:25,512
barely in his twenties,

151
00:09:25,552 --> 00:09:29,946
the son of a North West Company
partner and a Métis mother.

152
00:09:29,996 --> 00:09:30,751
That spring,

153
00:09:30,791 --> 00:09:35,590
Grant's men seized Hudson's Bay Company
pemmican on the Qu'Appelle River,

154
00:09:35,630 --> 00:09:37,405
hundreds of bags of it.

155
00:09:37,455 --> 00:09:40,757
Pemmican was now both
the prize and the weapon.

156
00:09:42,421 --> 00:09:47,643
In June, Grant's party, around sixty
riders, Métis and First Nations,

157
00:09:47,683 --> 00:09:50,540
set out to carry pemmican east to meet

158
00:09:50,580 --> 00:09:54,567
the North West Company's
canoes on Lake Winnipeg.

159
00:09:54,617 --> 00:09:57,295
To get there, they had to pass the colony.

160
00:09:57,345 --> 00:09:58,819
They cut across country,

161
00:09:58,859 --> 00:10:03,021
over a stretch of low ground the
Métis called la Grenouillère.

162
00:10:03,071 --> 00:10:04,325
Frog Plain.

163
00:10:05,889 --> 00:10:08,027
On the evening of June nineteenth,

164
00:10:08,067 --> 00:10:10,845
Governor Semple walked
out from Fort Douglas

165
00:10:10,885 --> 00:10:13,569
with about two dozen men to stop them.

166
00:10:15,733 --> 00:10:19,441
What happened next has been
argued over for two centuries.

167
00:10:19,491 --> 00:10:21,123
Words were exchanged.

168
00:10:21,353 --> 00:10:22,655
Someone fired.

169
00:10:22,885 --> 00:10:25,323
Each side said the other fired first.

170
00:10:25,373 --> 00:10:28,798
Within minutes, Semple and
twenty of his men were dead.

171
00:10:28,848 --> 00:10:32,497
On the Métis side, at least
one young man was killed.

172
00:10:36,661 --> 00:10:39,056
Grant's men went on to take Fort Douglas,

173
00:10:39,096 --> 00:10:42,197
and the remaining settlers
were forced to leave.

174
00:10:42,247 --> 00:10:46,728
For the second time in a little
over a year, the colony was emptied.

175
00:10:48,492 --> 00:10:53,079
In the settler tradition, the killings
became the Seven Oaks Massacre.

176
00:10:53,129 --> 00:10:58,975
For the Métis, it was the Victory of Frog
Plain, la Victoire de la Grenouillère.

177
00:10:59,025 --> 00:11:03,960
A Métis songwriter named Pierre Falcon
made a song about it that same year,

178
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,372
and it was sung for generations.

179
00:11:06,422 --> 00:11:09,628
Many historians, and
many Métis people today,

180
00:11:09,668 --> 00:11:15,329
see these years as a moment when the
Métis spoke of themselves as a nation.

181
00:11:17,293 --> 00:11:19,571
The killing settled nothing quickly.

182
00:11:19,621 --> 00:11:22,606
Lord Selkirk answered
with force of his own,

183
00:11:22,646 --> 00:11:25,287
seizing the North West Company's inland

184
00:11:25,327 --> 00:11:28,395
headquarters at Fort William that August.

185
00:11:28,445 --> 00:11:31,502
Lawsuits and inquiries
dragged on for years.

186
00:11:31,552 --> 00:11:35,071
Finally, under pressure
from the British government,

187
00:11:35,111 --> 00:11:37,262
the two rivals stopped fighting,

188
00:11:37,302 --> 00:11:41,659
and in 1821 they merged under
the Hudson's Bay Company name.

189
00:11:46,823 --> 00:11:48,733
And the pemmican kept moving.

190
00:11:48,783 --> 00:11:50,784
If anything, the trade grew.

191
00:11:50,834 --> 00:11:51,415
In 1840,

192
00:11:51,455 --> 00:11:54,289
a single summer hunt out of Red River

193
00:11:54,329 --> 00:11:57,997
set out with more than
twelve hundred carts.

194
00:11:58,047 --> 00:12:02,805
Later in the century, factory-made
pemmican went to the ends of the earth.

195
00:12:02,855 --> 00:12:07,455
In 1911, Roald Amundsen's team
carried it to the South Pole,

196
00:12:07,495 --> 00:12:11,379
in a version that added
oatmeal and dried peas.

197
00:12:12,943 --> 00:12:16,245
Which leaves the question
that pemmican still raises.

198
00:12:16,295 --> 00:12:19,597
A food this compact, this
durable, this proven.

199
00:12:19,647 --> 00:12:22,047
Why did it fade from everyday life?

200
00:12:23,611 --> 00:12:27,543
Part of the answer is canned
food and refrigeration.

201
00:12:27,593 --> 00:12:31,444
But on the plains, the deeper
answer is simpler, and harder.

202
00:12:31,494 --> 00:12:34,497
The animal it was made
from nearly disappeared.

203
00:12:36,061 --> 00:12:40,478
Nobody knows exactly how many
bison once lived on the plains.

204
00:12:40,528 --> 00:12:43,331
Estimates run into the tens of millions.

205
00:12:43,381 --> 00:12:46,361
Historians argue that a
century of feeding the

206
00:12:46,401 --> 00:12:50,000
fur trade had already worn
the northern herds down.

207
00:12:50,050 --> 00:12:51,449
Then, in the 1870s,

208
00:12:51,489 --> 00:12:56,219
new tanning methods turned bison
hides into industrial leather,

209
00:12:56,259 --> 00:13:00,901
and commercial hunters killed
the animals by the millions.

210
00:13:02,465 --> 00:13:08,231
By 1879, the bison were essentially
gone from the Canadian plains.

211
00:13:08,281 --> 00:13:11,585
Even the government's own
commissioner in the region

212
00:13:11,625 --> 00:13:15,476
admitted the disappearance
had taken Ottawa by surprise.

213
00:13:15,526 --> 00:13:20,551
Hunger followed, for the nations whose
lives had been built around the herds.

214
00:13:22,415 --> 00:13:23,046
In 1889,

215
00:13:23,086 --> 00:13:26,399
the naturalist William Hornaday tried to

216
00:13:26,439 --> 00:13:30,042
count every bison left in North America.

217
00:13:30,092 --> 00:13:33,133
His total was one thousand and ninety-one.

218
00:13:36,897 --> 00:13:41,068
The pemmican economy didn't
end because the idea failed.

219
00:13:41,118 --> 00:13:45,343
It ended because the world that
produced it was taken apart.

220
00:13:47,907 --> 00:13:53,001
Bison have slowly come back since then,
and pemmican never truly vanished.

221
00:13:53,051 --> 00:13:55,464
It's still made in Indigenous kitchens,

222
00:13:55,504 --> 00:13:58,924
by people who learned it the
way it was always learned:

223
00:13:58,964 --> 00:14:00,849
from someone who knew how.

224
00:14:00,899 --> 00:14:06,317
And in 2014, nations from both sides of
the border, among them the Blackfeet,

225
00:14:06,357 --> 00:14:09,507
the Blood Tribe, the
Siksika and the Piikani,

226
00:14:09,547 --> 00:14:12,413
signed what they call the Buffalo Treaty,

227
00:14:12,453 --> 00:14:16,035
a pact to bring the animal
back to their lands.

228
00:14:16,085 --> 00:14:20,787
It was the first treaty among them in
more than a hundred and fifty years.

229
00:14:23,151 --> 00:14:24,703
Look at the bag again.

230
00:14:24,753 --> 00:14:27,378
A governor tried to keep it in one valley.

231
00:14:27,428 --> 00:14:29,279
A war was fought around it.

232
00:14:29,329 --> 00:14:32,221
Explorers carried it to
the bottom of the world.

233
00:14:32,271 --> 00:14:37,418
But the knowledge inside it never belonged
to the colony, or to the companies.

234
00:14:37,468 --> 00:14:40,524
It belonged to the people
who learned how to turn

